                <pb facs="0517" n="437" sameAs="frus:frus1930v01/pg437"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">632.0023/5: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-10-15T16:00:00-05:00">October 15, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>64. Your 96 and 97 of October 14, 7 p.m., and October 15, 10 a.m. Department sent
                    following telegram No. 2 of October 14, 5 p.m., to Consul at Pernambuco: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“For your personal and confidential information, as Pernambuco is in the
                            hands of revolutionists, Department will not take steps to have ships
                            call there if the Brazilian authorities refuse to give them clearance
                            for that port. You will please be guided accordingly.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
